				<td colspan="2"><span class="postbody"><img src="http://mywebpage.netscape.com/murdockP4/kunarkgob.jpg" border="0" />
<br />

<br />
Snide drew a deep breath of the thin air of the Frontier Mountains.  His blades were black with the blood of goblins.  Outside a small cave mouth that led to three chambers, he sat quietly.  Hidden beneath his cloak next to a rock, he closed his eyes.  He was focusing his will to mend his wounds. Fighting the goblins for weeks, he was tired.  He had to res for a bit.  He was trying to gain favor with the Chardok royals, but second thoughts crept into his mind.  Sarnak used to fall to his blade, but now he wanted them to look at him as a comrade, an ally.  It all happened one day when he entered the Sarnak stronghold of Chardok.  
<br />

<br />
He entered to see what he could steal.  At the entrance was a diminutive Sarnak, a laughable pigmy.
<br />

<br />
	“Hail Shorty! Shouldn’t you be with your parents?”  Snide taunted the short one.
<br />

<br />
	“Oh, like I haven’t heard that one before,” the Herald scoffed.  “Lesser being are you here to improve yourself,  or just make jokes?  The Brood of Di’Zok can offer you a ring of power if you pledge allegiance to our king.”
<br />

<br />
	“I belong to no house or guild, and I bow before no one!”  With a tight grip, Snide held the Sarnak up by the neck and drew the blade that brought his rage.
<br />

<br />
	“Wait no! Stop, we can offer youmore!”
<br />

<br />
	“I’m listening,” Snide said gruffly, not putting his blade away.
<br />

<br />
	“Well.. the Que.. the Queen can make a powerful artifact for those that can slay our enemies.”
<br />

<br />
	“What is it?” the barbarian said through clenched teeth.
<br />

<br />
	“I… I.. I do not know.. it has many adventurers giving me goblin skins… and salts from the Frontier Mountains each day.  It must be pow.. powerful.”
<br />

<br />
“Why would I risk my life for you or your kind without knowing the reward?”  He yelled.
<br />

<br />
“I have heard the underlings say it has regenerative powers,” he said sniveling.  Then he shook his head.  “You should put me down, if I scream the guards will come for you,” he said suddenly angry and lucid.
<br />

<br />
Snide laughed, dropping the Herald to the ground roughly, and putting the Ragebringer into his belt.  “I could cut your throat fast enough and sneak out of this foul smelling grotto as well.”
<br />

<br />
“The Di’Zok will not let you in Chardok now.  You have killed many Sarnak berserkers in the Overthere.  I have heard of you, rogue.  And if you try to venture in our city our chokidai will smell you out.”
<br />

<br />
“I got in well enough,” Snide turned around to walk out, but he saw there was no exit where he had entered.  There was a solid wall.  He felt the rock for a crack or an opening but there was none was to be found.
<br />

<br />
“What the hell?”  Snide drew his rage again and pointed it at the short Dragonkin.  “How do I get out of here, you miserable retch?”
<br />

<br />
“You have to go across our gates, and past many chokidai before you get to the exit door,” he chuckled.
<br />

<br />
Snide drew himself up to lunge, but then thought better of it.  He sighed, and then frowned.  It was times like this that he wished had a clockwork talisman.  He had gathered many pieces but still could not find any bile-based ooze amongst the planar beings.  Luckily he had something just as good as the talisman with him.  Deep in his backpack was a hammer that he hardly used, except for emergencies.  It was smelted with a gate spell pointing to the docks of the Overthere.  It originally used to send the skeletal boat workers back to the docks if they were in another location, but it served him as an escape device.  Snide swung the hammer at Telcha.  The only problem with the hammer was that one had to swing it in battle for the gate spell to work.  He struck Telcha many times, and the Herald cried out as he was pounded by the large hammer.  Snide wasn’t very good with the hammer, so it only did minimal damage.
<br />

<br />
“Does this damn thing even fu— ,” as he spoke the hammer glowed and Telcha tried to duck but the hammer hit him in the forhead, knocking him backwards.  
<br />

<br />
Snide laughed, his voice ringing loudly in the Herald’s ears as it bounced off the walls.  His voice echoed, as he disappeared in a wisp of smoke and he traveled to the Outpost in the Overthere.
<br />

<br />
continued next week...</span><span class="gensmall"><br /><br />Last edited by Snide on Thu Apr 22, 2004 7:30 pm; edited 1 time in total</span></td>
