			<tr>
				<td colspan="2"><span class="postbody"><img src="http://mywebpage.netscape.com/murdockP4/icy.jpg" border="0" />
<br />

<br />
Snide found the company of his group lacking in personality.  The druid particularly irked him.  The elf didn’t seem to comprehend his role; he couldn’t heal, and had trouble snaring the goblins' feet, all in all a failure.  And the paladin, oh the paladin... had Snide cursing the whole adventure!
<br />

<br />
“You'd have just as good luck shouting ‘We’re here come and get us!’,” Snide said angrily.  “I'm surprised the entire Menagerie wasn't emptied on that pull.”  His anger simmered, and the cleric gated to save his own skin as well as his sanity, that is when Snide ran to the exit, abandoning his so called ‘comrades.’  <span style="font-style: italic">Damn fools</span>, he thought to himself.  Snide entered the portal and was transported to the tower above Miragul’s Menagerie.  Snide walked over the ice and snow, despondent.  He heard some footsteps and put his hand to his weapon.  From behind a Barbarian woman ran past, “Help me,” she yelled, a snow leopard was chasing her; she looked bloodied, near death.
<br />

<br />
Snide would have let her die, it was no concern of him, but she caught his eye.  Something about her was irresistible.  Snide pulled out a throwing star from a secret compartment in his bracer and threw it at the leopard.  It hit with deadly accuracy at the cat’s neck, and it fell face first into a snow bank.  Megan O’Reily turned to see who her savior was.
<br />

<br />
“Thank you so much, sir?”  She asked falling on her knees.
<br />
As Snide drew closer he grinned.
<br />

<br />
Her face became pale, “You are one of the bandits!” she got up to run but Snide grabbed her hand.
<br />

<br />
“Help!  Tundra Ja—,“ she was cut off as Snide covered her mouth and dragged her to one of the many ruins in the Everfrost.
<br />

<br />
"Why are did you scream, I’m not gonna hurt you,” he said, keeping his hand over her mouth.  Fiercly, she bit his hand.  With a yelp, he slapped her. Tears flowed freely down her cheeks. Snide tried kissing her, but she struggled, kicking and hitting him. Then she punched him in the face. "You!" he yelled. He brought his his hands over her neck. Her eyes pleaded with him, but he kept his hold strong. She beat at him, but he held fast. Then the gleam from her eyes was gone, and she went limp. 
<br />

<br />
Unfortunately no one heard the cries.  He left the ruins and began the short run back to the Plane of Knowledge book.  But he wouldn’t reach it.  There was a strange snowstorm that picked up suddenly, covering the land in white.  You wouldn’t be able to see a foot in front of you if you could keep your eyes open, with the wind and snow blowing into your face.  The Barbarians of Halas, hadn’t seen much like it before.  Snide struggled against the wind trying to find his way along.  Before he could react, down he fell, nearly six feet onto his leg.  He must have got reversed somehow; he was on the river again.  The ice cracked when he hit.  The sound of his leg cracking worried him more though.  It immediately caused Snide to cry out in pain.
<br />

<br />
Snide lay there, as the blizzard seemed to wane just as fast as it came on.  When he could see again he slowly tried to stand up.  His leg collapsed underneath him and the ice as well.
<br />
“Gahh!” he screamed, and fell into the water, his arms flailing, and his leg limp.  As his heavy armor dragged him down, the icy cold penetrated his body.</span><span class="gensmall"></span></td>
