				<td colspan="2"><span class="postbody"><img src="http://mywebpage.netscape.com/murdockP4/cember.jpg" border="0" />
<br />

<br />
Cemberfrost felt the cold chill of the air against his lungs as he breathed.  With a sigh he heaved a backpack onto the ground to rest, when he heard a yell.  It was just as the blizzard that had lost him in his homeland abated.  It was a quite strange blizzard.  He turned in the direction of the river, where he heard the scream.  People often drowned in the Everfrost River, trapped beneath the ice, unable to find an opening.  The red bearded Barbarian trudged along in his boots spraying the new snow.  Cemberfrost Chillwind put his hands to his hips and gazed over the river looking for any sign of distress.  There on the river was a cracked section of ice and a hand reached up from the water.  The hand sank quickly, as Cemberfrost slid down the embankment, careful not to fall.  After readying himself, Cemberfrost swam down beneath the ice, closing his eyes.  As he opened his eyes, he felt the sharp pain of the cold water, but he could see a man below. He dove down and pulled the man in chainmail up to the cracks of the ice.  With a grunt he pushed the man onto the ice and then hoisted himself out.
<br />

<br />
“Snide?” he said incredulously.  “I barely recognized you.” Snide didn’t say anything, his eyes were closed and he shivered.  “How did you fall under the ice?”  Snide answered slowly.
<br />

<br />
“I ff f—ell,” Snide shivered.
<br />

<br />
“The ice is very thick though.  And I thought rogues knew how to fall safely?”
<br />

<br />
“There was a storm.”
<br />

<br />
“Aye, most unfortunate for you.  Let’s get you to somewhere warm.  The Wayfarer camp, stand up.”
<br />

<br />
“Can- can’t.  Legs brr—broke.”
<br />

<br />
“I’ll help you,” Cemberfrost put his arm around Snide’s shoulder, and they walked, Snide limping along on good leg, using Cemberfrost for support.  “What are you doing in Everfrost?” Cemberfrost asked.
<br />

<br />
“I just got back from M- mmiragul’s Menagerie,” Snide frowned.  “I think I met the worst group ever.  The sh- shaman was a numbskull.  He went in front of the scout and drew all sorts of m- monsters on us.”
<br />

<br />
“That bad huh?”
<br />

<br />
Snide nodded sadly.  When they reached the Wayfarer camp, Snide slumped next to the fire.  
<br />

<br />
“We have to get you warm, your clothing is freezing,” he said.  Cemberfrost covered Snide with a bear skin after stripping off his armor and clothing.  Snide looked very pale and his leg looked quite bad.  It was a compound fracture in his tibia and his knee was badly bruised.  “Lord Fubarz, can you help Snide here?”
<br />
A High Elf opened his eyes and stood from his meditations to notice the freezing Barbarian at the fire.
<br />

<br />
“What happened?” He said, concerned.
<br />

<br />
“I fell.”  Snide said simply.  “I swear I have the worst luck, t- terrible groups, that woman…”
<br />

<br />
“What woman?"
<br />

<br />
“Oh s- some Barbarian wench I killed,” he said, almost casually.
<br />

<br />
“Hmmm?”  Cemberfrost raised his eyebrows.
<br />

<br />
“Oh yeah and Jack too.”  Snide spoke the last sentence softly, almost under his breath.
<br />

<br />
“What?”  Cemberfrost said, his eyebrows furrowing.
<br />

<br />
“I killed Tundra Jack, and his bear.  I h- hated him so much.  Defender of his homeland.  A place I have never been welcome in.  Always hunted, just for my past with the Karana bandits.”
<br />

<br />
“Tundra Jack was a good man, you have erred much to slay him.  He must have been taken unawares for
<br />
you to accomplish such a feat,” he said with iron in his voice.
<br />

<br />
“He was fleeing.  I killed him while he was fleeing.  Quite easy,” Snide said.  “Jack the brave, Jack the just.”
<br />

<br />
“Tundra Jack was one of the White Wolves, the warriors of Halas, as am I.”  Cemberfrost had raised his voice.  Snide was startled.
<br />

<br />
“So?”
<br />

<br />
“You dare slay the protectors of Everfrost?"  Cemberfrost took a staff from his back.  The top was covered in a steel circle, tied with a large horn.  "How could I have been so blind as to allow you to roam freely in the Halls of Domus Umbra?"
<br />

<br />
“Cemberfrost? What are you saying, I thought I was your friend?”
<br />

<br />
The warrior whirled the staff over his head, "The Tribunal need judge you for your fell deeds.”  As the staff crushed down, a thunderous roar filled Snide’s mind.  When the staff hit his forehead he screamed “No!”  There was a flash of light that drew from the staff to the sky, and then Snide felt his consciousness crushed into oblivion.</span><span class="gensmall"></span></td>
