				<td colspan="2"><span class="postbody"><img src="http://mywebpage.netscape.com/murdockP4/seventh.jpg" border="0" />
<br />

<br />
Snide felt so lost.  Without any sight or sound, all that drew his thought was thought itself.  It was as if his consciousness was drifting on the wind or through the ocean.  Time seemed relative, and he could find no way to comprehend its passing.  No feeling came from his body; he couldn’t open his eyes or shrug his shoulders.  Did he even have a body? Was he dead?
<br />

<br />
Snide could barely remember what had happened to him.  All he could remember was dark and loneliness.  Life seemed full of it, and now whatever this was… this afterlife or strange consciousness was completely dark.  No, not dark.  That was the wrong word.  It was nothing, oblivion, only his mind.  It took him a long time to find some flow of thought that made much sense, he supposed he should find out was this was, somehow investigate this.  What felt like an eternity past, with Snide gaining no insight to his condition.  He had lost all feeling of the world.  He must be dead, how could there be any other explanation?  Maybe he was having some terrible dream?  No, he never had dreams like this.  This was hardly like those dreams of falling, or the dream of being naked in a room full of people.  
<br />

<br />
He focused, accepting the facts.  He was either paralyzed, in some sort of a coma, or he was dead.  Priest constantly talked of life with their Gods and how living a faithful life may bring you to your God to serve them forever.  But what if you had no God?  What if you shunned them all, decided to kill freely in their planes without regard to them?  Was he to remain forever in this nameless nothing?  The Nameless….  Snide supposed that if his mind wasn’t gone he must be simply paralyzed, for if he really had died his mind would be destroyed as well, for certainly the mind couldn’t exist without the body.
<br />

<br />
Snide was mistaken though, he was technically dead, and it was with the Nameless that he now resided.  Deep within the force that created the very universe, Snide’s unfaithful and Godless mind swam slowly and without regard to time.  The rogue would never know or could never comprehend his position, but it wouldn’t matter for through the oblivion he sped, through the Nameless’s boundless energy and there he started to get a sense of self.  His sight was returned, and a glow came to his eyes as he slowly realized he had eyes.  He looked down at his body to see a glowing shell of his former self.  Pinpricks of light appeared in the blackness ahead.  As Snide gazed around he was shocked to notice a dark figure standing next him.
<br />

<br />
A man, cloaked in black, floated next to him, his face hidden.  He carried a large hammer in his right hand.  The cloaked man motioned with his hammer up and Snide felt his will float through the ether of the Nameless and the Planes of Power.  Snide’s sight went black and then slowly faded into focus.  He stood naked except for a bear skin over his shoulders.  The platform on which he stood was black, an ebony color so dark that it seemed to swallow light.  Above he could see the stars, although they made no reflection on the smooth platform.  
<br />

<br />
	Six giant beings stood around him in a large circle.  Their faces were impossible to discern beneath their black hoods.  They wore black holocaust cloaks around their powerful forms, and each held a giant gavel wrought with gold in their black gloves.
<br />

<br />
	The Six Hammers…</span><span class="gensmall"></span></td>
