<br />

<br />
Snide drew his hand across his head, feeling his shorn hair.  The dreadlocks and ratty hair was gone, replaced by short hair on top, which was much cleaner.  He had washed in Lake Rivervale, after buying a drink from the halflings’ bar.  His head was wanted in the small Halfling city, but it was much too high for most of them to take.  With  the secret handshake though, he could buy from anyone.  Just a sneak behind and a tap on the shoulder was all it took.  With his Ragebringer wet Snide began scraping at his cheeks and chin with the razor sharp blade, removing all the hair from his face, save a small strip on his upper lip.  Bending his head down, he looked at his reflection in the lake.  He could barely recognize the man he saw, so much had happened since his <span style="font-style: italic">vision</span>. 
<br />

<br />
Lon had sent him on a wild goose chase.  All across Norrath he had gathered clues.  The Gem of Stamina and the Gem of Righteousness were what he sought, but it turned out that getting those items involved the collection of quite a few other items, including a collection of exotic ales.  He had fought many creatures and ran across the land.  It was a huge undertaking, but Snide felt stronger as he began to complete the quest.  
<br />

<br />
Snide put his clothes back on and rubbed his face, feeling the clean surface.  Then, skulking along he walked to Misty Thicket and the Plane of Knowledge book.  Before he was able to get the last bit for the collection, Snide knew he had to train intensely.  His leg felt much better; only when it snowed could he feel any pain now.  Over many days and weeks he increased his strength and agility.  He lifted boulders over his head that giants used as throwing weapons to increase the size of his arms and even practiced his aim against the ice giants of Everfrost.  Soon his back and arms were so strong that he was lifting twice what he could before the accident.  Balance and agility were a bit tougher.  Barbarians were known for their great strength, but it took much work to become dexterous as well as agile.  Snide practiced balancing daggers on his fingers.  It was difficult for him to maintain patience, but he knew that if he was to pass the coming tests ahead, he would need to be stronger and faster than before.  His daggers flew through the air so fast that he was able to cut a glass bottle if someone threw it into the air.  Falling without damage became another obsession as the rogue continued to roll and fall, trying to perfect the techniques of safe falling.  After nearly two months of intense training, Snide felt he was as prepared as he could ever be.
<br />

<br />
Last on his list for the Gem of Stamina, was blood wine.  Just the sound of the alcohol conjured visions of vampires.  He knew it was Mistmoore that he needed to travel, for no where else on Norrath was the presence of vampires so strong and organized enough to make something as macabre as blood wine.  Traveling to the vampires was simple enough; a quick jaunt through the Plane of Knowledge to Greater Faydark, and Snide was on his way to Lord Mistmoore’s dark Castle.
<br />

<br />
The vampires were quite lax in their defenses.  After the veil between Luclin and Norrath was lifted people hardly visited the Castle to slay the undead.  With careful steps, Snide moved along the outer courtyard, until he saw his opportunity to move behind the vampire sentries.  If only he could find a wine cellar or something that would hint the location of blood wine.  The rogue skulked up a large ramp to the entrance of Mistmoore castle.  A small bridge connected the large ramp to the Castle’s front, but two vampiric guards stood there, making no sounds and hardly moving.  Snide went to the far side of the cliff on the ramp and climbed down.  Being careful to keep strong handholds, he climbed across the rock wall, until he reached the side of the bridge.  Then he heard a click as the front door opened.  A dark elf strode out, tall and gaunt.  Sharp pointed canines pressed against his lower lip, and his long hair was pulled back behind his head.  Snide had noticed several different kinds of vampires in Mistmoore Caverns but most of these in the Castle seemed to be Orphans or Sustainers, those that had been turned from living elves into vampires, and not Trueborn, or vampires that claimed to have Mistmoore’s blood flowing in their veins.
<br />

<br />
Snide clung to crevices in the old stonework on the side of the bridge, hiding from the lurking vampire.  The click of the vampire’s boots against the stone faded as he crossed the bridge and walked down the ramp.  Pulling himself over the railing, Snide got back onto the bridge quietly behind the guards, and entered the castle.  The halls were dark, and musty, one could smell death and blood in the air.  In the shadows, Snide skulked, along the hallways, peering into the rooms of the castle.  He opened a door slowly, but it creaked.  He stopped opening the door and froze.  From inside he heard a piano, but suddenly the music stopped and there was a preternatural hiss.  The door was suddenly open and a female dark elf in a long purple robe stood in its place.  She looked down the hallway, her red eyes almost glowing with intent.  Snide held his breath, from above her.  He pressed his body against the rafters, trying to stay steady.  The vampire walked down the hall, and Snide dropped down, hanging from the rafters for half a second before he landed silently.  He quickly moved into the next room.  The piano was large, and its open top almost hit the roof.  The room was small but had windows opening out into the inner courtyard.  Snide opened the courtyard door and skulked across to the thick stone wall surrounding the area.  A few vampires walked the grounds, including a hemo enologist.  She held a wine glass in one hand and a bottle of dark wine in the other.  Another vampire was drinking with her.  Silently Snide drew his ragebringer from his belt and stabbed the enologist cleanly through the heart.  The bottle of blood wine and the glass fell from her hands as she clutched her chest.  With blinding speed the rogue caught the falling bottle of wine, placing it into his satchel, the other vampire rushing at him.  The undead predator slashed at his neck, as he drew his rage from the dead enologist.  Snide pressed his knife into the vampire’s cold flesh again and again, as it tried to punch him, until it collapsed on the courtyard, black blood staining ground.
<br />

<br />
After wiping his blades on the enologists robes, Snide crawled up the courtyard wall and left the castle, his prize won.  When Snide returned all the spirits, the Gem of Stamina was his.  The Gem of Righteousness though, was a much easier task to obtain.  In the dregs of Freeport, Snide found a Locked Gem Case.  He couldn’t pick the lock, but there were three keys to open it.  Bubar in the East Commonlands had one, Toxdil in West Freeport had one, and Ms. Sparkle in the West Commonlands had the last one.  Through trickery, Snide traded a useless gem for Toxdil’s key, and Ms. Sparkle wanted a purse that was stolen from her for her key.  Deep in Solusek’s Eye was where it was; a dark elf mage had stolen it.  One quick stab and it was returned to its rightful owner.  Snide gave his two keys and the gem case to Bubar, who used his own key as well as the others to open it.  Inside was the Gem of Righteousness.
<br />

<br />
With the gems in hand, Snide told the Magus in East Commonlands that he needed to get to South Roand the Magus obliged.  In the Southern Desert of Ro, in the Northwest corner, Snide gave the orc the two gems he was promised.  Orthalius drew a sword from behind himself, almost out of thin air and set it gently in Snide’s hands.  It was the Burning Rapier.  It was a symbol of those rogues redeemed.  Snide never thought his soul could be pure, but he felt a warmth deep in his heart growing.  Something was changing inside him, he felt nervous, as if even greater challenges lay before him.</span><span class="gensmall"></span></td>
