				<td colspan="2"><span class="postbody"><span style="font-style: italic">Sorry for the delay in posting this one, it took quite a bit of doing, I had to get a lot of information from Cemberfrost and permissions from Meneltel and Ellene.  I'm not sure if I put everything in the correct perspective and I still am not quite happy with it, but I have a lot of story to plow through, so I can't really keep putting this off.  The show must go on, hopefully i'll be back to my 6 day schedual.</span>
<br />

<br />
<img src="http://mywebpage.netscape.com/murdockP4/rapier.jpg" border="0" />
<br />

<br />
	Orthalius had a message for Snide.  He took a small letter from a pouch on his belt, and handed it to Snide.
<br />

<br />
	“A bard gave me this to give to you.”
<br />

<br />
	The seal of Altus Concilium was on the back.  Snide tore off the side of the envelope quickly and pulled out a small sheet of paper.   It was brief.
<br />
	
<br />
Snide:
<br />

<br />
Your attendance is required at Halas in the halls of Domus Umbra.
<br />

<br />
Signed, Cemberfrost Chillwind
<br />

<br />
	Snide put the message in his backpack and began running south towards the Wayfarers’ camp.  The dark elf magus waved him goodbye as he said, “Butcherblock Mountains.”  The translocation was fast.  Snide took off west from the camp in Butcherblock across the path and then north towards Kaladim.  Through the use of the Plane of Knowledge stones he was able to get to the Everfrost Peaks much faster than running across country.  Snide hid in the shadows as he approached Halas.  He had only tried to enter the city once, but the guards looked at him threateningly.  But with his superior skills, Snide covered himself in a shroud of stealth.  He plunged into the icy water and swam across to the dock.  He hoisted himself up slowly and then skulked past the guards to the arena where the warrior of Halas fought.  
<br />

<br />
The Halls of Domus Umbra were shortly beyond the arena past a small gate.  Inside the gate was Cemberfrost.  He didn’t sit and read like Lord Hylphae used to, this Lord of Umbra was embroiled in action.  Domus Umbra had its own arena and that is where Cemberfrost was positioned.  Steel was joined fiercely as a young recruit of Umbra spared with Cemberfrost.  It was only twenty seconds before the young man was disarmed.  Cemberfrost smiled and looked over the human’s head.  The two Barbarian’s eyes met and Cemberfrost walked out of the arena towards Snide.  He welcomed Snide with a strongly clasped handshake and then sent a messenger to get the other houses of Dark Inheritance.  The bard quickly left and went off to get the leaders of the other guilds.
<br />
It took several days before people started arriving.  The Marshals arrived last.
<br />

<br />
	Snide bowed before Serrik and Raiya Ryuujin.  They confirmed his suspicions that his application had been accepted, and that he could once again be with the Dark Inheritance.  Cemberfrost would take Snide’s oaths, being leader of Domus Umbra.  He then told the Marshals of his quest, and they reported to him, that he would have to contact the members of Dark Inheritance individually, they could not help him.  Snide nodded gravely, thanking them for their counsel and bid his leave to stand in front of the red-bearded barbarian.  All of Domus Umbra was joined at the large meeting room, along with most of Domus Solis and a few members of Domus Noctis, most notably Melentel.  
<br />

<br />
Snide knelt and took the oath that Cemberfrost spoke loudly.  When the ceremony was finished, the old rogue stood and grinned from ear to ear, as the warmth within his heart grew.  It was then that Snide may have dared a bit too much.  He turned and faced the gathering, speaking out about his quest and the help he would need to gain access to the cities of Norrath.  He needed those in very good favor to their city and their leaders.
<br />

<br />
“The Tribunal has planned for me to take up this burden and tell the cities of the world what is in store for them if they continue their ways.”
<br />

<br />
“I already know you’re thinking thief, and you can forget about it,” Meneltel said caustically.  “There are no rightful rulers in Neriak now, and I won’t help you there.” 
<br />

<br />
Snide didn’t say anything.  Then someone in the back spoke out, although Snide didn’t recognize the voice.
<br />

<br />
“Why should we help you?  You haven’t helped us at all when you were in the guild before. You never did any raids with us.”
<br />

<br />
“Didn’t you just get a Ragebringer and then leave us?” someone else said.
<br />

<br />
Snide withdrew the Ragebringer from his belt and threw it point down into the ground, with a scowl.
<br />

<br />
“No.  I left Dark Inheritance long ago when I didn’t know the meaning of friendship.  I was guildless for such a long time, with only my fellow rogues to keep me company.  I called in debts from many people to help me to obtain that Rage,” he said as he pointed to the hilt of the dagger.  “People I knew long before Dark Inheritance and those I knew during my tenure as well,” he said with emphasis.  “And as for raids, when I was a member of Dark Inheritance, there was never an opportune moment to attend.  It has only been of late that I have been able to help out.  I have been with you and Descending Essence in the Plane of Fear and in the Overthere to slay the Cliff Golem.  Have you forgotten those things?”
<br />

<br />
There was silence.
<br />

<br />
“I don’t ask for your forgiveness for I have done no wrong to you in my mind.  My only wish is that you would think of me with an open heart without judgment, for I am a different person that I was before.  I have been redeemed by Ro.”  
<br />

<br />
It was at this point that Snide drew a rapier from his Rallic Pack.  When he held the sword up in the air it flickered in flame.  There were whispers in the crowd, and then the rogue lowered his sword.
<br />

<br />
“If anyone would help me, please see me later.  Thank you,” Snide said, bowing his head.  After picking up his dagger he placed it back in his belt and walked to a corner.  Cemberfrost stood in front of those gathered from Domus Umbra and the other houses and thanked them for coming to the ceremony.
<br />

<br />
People began to disperse and go their separate ways, some talking about current events and some leaving.  A woman in a white hooded cloak approached Snide.  With a toss of her head she threw back her hood and her beautiful blond hair cascaded across her shoulders.
<br />

<br />
“Hey big guy how are you?”  Ellene kissed Snide on the lips and gave him a hug.
<br />

<br />
“Not bad girl,” Snide said, trying to cheer up.
<br />

<br />
“I’ll help you if you need it.  Freeport is my hometown, and if you need me there, I’ll be happy to go.”  She said with a big grin.
<br />

<br />
“Thank you very much,” Snide said.  Then he looked up above Ellene.
<br />

<br />
“I will help as well.”
<br />

<br />
Snide moved forward and Ellene stepped to the side so that Cemberfrost and Snide could stand together.  With firm grips they shook hands.
<br />

<br />
“I’ll take you before the Clan Leaders when you need it.”
<br />

<br />
“Thank you,” Snide said, as he bowed his head.
<br />

<br />
	“Where will you start, here in Halas?”
<br />

<br />
	All the cities of Norrath and even Shar Vahl on Luclin… Snide started to fathom the length of his quest.  Cabalis and Halas would be the most difficult cities to convince, Qeynos as well.  For much of his young life Snide was in the employ of the Karana bandits.  There he built a bad reputation with Halas and Qeynos.  Snide didn’t have to think long about where to head first.  Freeport would be first on the list.  He was well tolerated there.
<br />

<br />
	“Freeport.”
<br />

<br />
	“Woo hoo!” Ellene cheered, she jumped onto Snide hugging him.
<br />

<br />
	“Please girl, not now,” he said gently brushing her off.
<br />

<br />
	“I’m sorry I’m just proud of Freeport,” she said with a smile.  “And I’m glad I get to be first.”
<br />

<br />
	Snide looked around for more to approach, but none did.  He overheard so many talk about how busy they were, and soon the gathering hall was empty.</span><span class="gensmall"><br /><br />Last edited by Snide on Fri Jun 18, 2004 10:35 pm; edited 1 time in total</span></td>
